{
 "thread": "i9MywzNRVds",
 "subject": "Estimates for correlated exogenous variables",
 "messages_listed": 3,
 "last_activity": "2016-08-10T16:05:46Z",
 "messages": [
  {
   "id": "P1S8u3KTAQAJ",
   "from": "Tabea Schoeler",
   "author": "7303fe6880f86e61",
   "date": "2016-08-09T11:49:08Z",
   "subject": "Estimates for correlated exogenous variables",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">\r\n\t\r\n\t\r\n\t\r\n\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">Hello all,</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><br>\r\n\r\n</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">I have the following\r\nreciprocal panel model, in which I want to get an estimate for\r\ncorrelated exogenous variables (<span lang=\"en-AU\">relapse_Y1\r\n~~ can_Y1</span>), the two autoregressive paths (<span lang=\"en-AU\">relapse_Y1\r\npredicts relapse_Y2 and can_Y1 predicts can_Y2) and the reciprocal\r\neffects between relapse_Y2 and can_Y2. Two covariates are controlled\r\nfor (meds, drugs).</span></font></p>\r\n<p lang=\"en-AU\" style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><br>\r\n\r\n</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">M2 &lt;- &#39;</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">    # Lagged path</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">      can_Y2 ~\r\nrelapse_Y2 \r\n</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">      relapse_Y2 ~\r\ncan_Y2</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">    # Autoregressive\r\npath</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">      can_Y2 ~\r\ncan_Y1 \r\n</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">      relapse_Y2 ~\r\nrelapse_Y1</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">    # Covariates</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">      can_Y2 ~ meds\r\n+ drugs \r\n</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">      relapse_Y2 ~\r\nmeds + drugs \r\n</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">    # correlated\r\nexogenous variables</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">      relapse_Y1 ~~\r\ncan_Y1&#39;</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><br>\r\n\r\n</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">M2_FIT &lt;-\r\nlavaan::sem(M2, data=SEM, estimator=&quot;WLSMV&quot;,\r\nordered=names(SEM), std.ov=FALSE) ##fit the model</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><br>\r\n\r\n</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">summary(M2_FIT,\r\nfit.measures = TRUE,standardized=TRUE, rsq=TRUE)</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">fitMeasures(M2_FIT,\r\nc(&quot;chisq&quot;,&quot;df&quot; ,&quot;pvalue&quot;  ,&quot;cfi&quot;\r\n,&quot;rmsea&quot;))</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><br>\r\n\r\n</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">With the correlation\r\nterm (r<b>elapse_Y1 ~~ can_Y1</b>), I get the error message “<b>lavaan ERROR:\r\nmodel syntax contains variance/covariance/intercept formulas\r\ninvolving (an) exogenous variable(s): [can_Y1 relapse_Y1]; Please\r\nremove them and try again.</b>” \r\n</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><br>\r\n\r\n</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">I am aware that I\r\ndon’t have to include this term since the effect is partialed out\r\nanyway. However, I would like to bring it into the model to be able\r\nto report an estimate.</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><br>\r\n\r\n</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">From what I\r\nunderstood it seems that I have to upgrade those variables to latent\r\nvariables?</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><br>\r\n\r\n</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">I tried the\r\nfollowing command, in which I upgraded can_Y1/ relapse_Y1, but I only\r\nget an error message (Error in tmp[cbind(REP$row[idx], REP$col[idx])]\r\n&lt;- lavpartable$free[idx] : NAs are not allowed in subscripted\r\nassignments)</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><br>\r\n\r\n</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><br>\r\n\r\n</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">M2 &lt;- &#39;</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\"># upgrading\r\nexogenous variables to latents</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">      frelapse_Y1 =~\r\n1*relapse_Y1</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">      fcan_Y1 =~\r\n1*can_Y1</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">    # Lagged path</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">      can_Y2 ~\r\nrelapse_Y2 \r\n</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">      relapse_Y2 ~\r\ncan_Y2</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">    # Autoregressive\r\npath</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">      can_Y2 ~\r\ncan_Y1 \r\n</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">      relapse_Y2 ~\r\nrelapse_Y1</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">    # Covariates</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">      can_Y2 ~ meds\r\n+ drugs \r\n</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">      relapse_Y2 ~\r\nmeds + drugs&#39;</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><br>\r\n\r\n</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><br>\r\n\r\n</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">Would you have any\r\nadvice?</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><br>\r\n\r\n</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">Many thanks \r\n</font></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">Tabea</font></p></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "yLwkRRCaAQAJ",
   "from": "Terrence Jorgensen",
   "author": "3fe90052fbf2f899",
   "date": "2016-08-09T13:50:21Z",
   "subject": "Re: Estimates for correlated exogenous variables",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><span style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;line-height:100%\">Would you have any\r\nadvice?</span></p></div></blockquote><div><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>If they are exogenous variables, you don&#39;t need to model their correlation, you can just calculate it using a polychoric correlation estimator (e.g.,<font face=\"courier new, monospace\"> lavCor()</font>, or<font face=\"courier new, monospace\"> polychoric() </font>from the psych package).</div><div><br></div><div>If you want to do the pseudo-latent variable method, you need to make those your regressors, don&#39;t keep using the &quot;indicators&quot; as regressors.  And you should probably move the regressands into the latent space, too (that&#39;s what lavaan would have to do behind the curtain anyway to estimate those regression paths).</div><div><br></div><div><div>Terrence D. Jorgensen</div><div>Postdoctoral Researcher, Methods and Statistics</div><div>Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam</div><div>UvA web page: <a href=\"http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen\" style=\"color:rgb(17,85,204)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792428184218000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3KNBhY6T5nMwZhXCiQNwkf\">http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen</a></div></div><div><br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "6_XCbAjwAQAJ",
